<.guide_page
  title={@guide_meta.title}
  dek={@guide_meta.dek}
  date={@guide_meta.date}
  read_time={@guide_meta.read_time}
>
  <p>
    Give a coding agent a terminal and a job, and it can get surprisingly far with tools
    you already have. It runs a command, reads the result, pipes it through something else,
    writes a small script, and keeps going. Nobody had to build a special AI integration
    for <code>grep</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then we give the same agent a long menu of MCP tools and make it work through them one
    call at a time. Fetch these records. Read them. Pick the interesting ones. Call another
    tool. Read those results too. Somewhere in the middle, a model that can write a perfectly
    good program has become the person manually copying cells between spreadsheets.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's the appeal of CLI tools for AI agents. They give an agent room to compose a solution,
    and they let ordinary programs do the parts that don't need a language model.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose">Why developers are choosing CLI tools for agents</h2>
  <p>
    Peter Steinberger, <a href="https://x.com/steipete">@steipete</a> on X, put it bluntly
    in his October 2025 workflow post:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Almost all MCPs really should be clis.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    In <a href="https://steipete.me/posts/just-talk-to-it#what-about-mcps">Just Talk To It</a>,
    he describes using familiar command-line tools and letting an agent read help when it
    needs it, instead of loading a large tool catalog into the conversation. His example is
    GitHub: why introduce another interface when the agent can already use <code>gh</code>?
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason is easy to recognize. A terminal agent already has a way to run
    programs, inspect files, and recover from errors. A CLI fits into that working environment
    without asking it to learn a separate interaction pattern for each service.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compose">The useful part is what happens between commands</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose you want a short list of open pull requests, leaving out drafts. An agent
    could fetch a page of results, read every entry, discard the drafts, and write out the
    remaining numbers and titles. Or it could ask the computer to do that:
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Filter pull requests before the agent reads them">set -o pipefail

gh pr list --repo OWNER/REPO --state open --limit 100 \
  --json number,title,isDraft |
  jq -r '.[] | select(.isDraft == false) | [.number, .title] | @tsv'</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Replace <code>OWNER/REPO</code> with a repository you can read. This Bash pipeline needs
    an authenticated GitHub CLI and <code>jq</code>; it checks up to 100 open pull requests,
    not the entire history. GitHub documents the available fields in its <a href="https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_pr_list">pull-request CLI reference</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model gets a small list to reason about. The JSON parsing and filtering happen
    outside its context window. Need to compare that list with a local release checklist?
    Save it to a file and write a few lines of Python. Need the same check tomorrow? Keep
    the script. You don't need somebody to build a tool for that exact combination.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is where a narrow MCP wrapper can get in the way. It exposes the operations and
    arguments its author chose. If the underlying CLI has a useful flag the wrapper doesn't
    expose, the agent has to find a workaround or wait for the wrapper to catch up. With the
    CLI, it can use the available commands and combine them with the rest of the environment.
    The account's permissions still apply.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tokens">Where the token savings come from</h2>
  <p>
    There are two separate costs here. First, the agent needs to learn which tools exist and
    how to call them. A client that puts every tool definition into the prompt pays that
    context cost before the work begins. A CLI can let the agent read the relevant help as
    it goes. Help text still costs tokens when the agent reads it, but unrelated commands
    don't have to come along for the ride.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, there's the data moving between tools. If a command returns ten thousand log
    lines and the next step is to count failures by service, a program can do the counting.
    The agent needs the counts and a few representative failures. Sending it the whole log
    first spends context on a job the computer already knows how to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    A script can also handle a loop, combine several read-only checks, and stop on an error
    without asking the model to choose every next call. That can reduce model round trips
    as well as the amount of text it reads. The model still decides what to investigate and
    what the findings mean. It just doesn't have to supervise every row.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anthropic described the same problem in <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/code-execution-with-mcp">Code execution with MCP</a>.
    Its November 2025 example reduced tool-loading context from 150,000 tokens to 2,000,
    a 98.7% reduction, by loading tool definitions on demand. It also describes processing
    intermediate results in code before returning selected information to the model.
    That example used MCP underneath. It demonstrates why the execution pattern matters;
    it is not a benchmark showing that every CLI is 98.7% cheaper than MCP.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can lose the advantage by dumping huge command output straight back into the chat.
    You can also spend it on repeated guesses at undocumented flags. A useful agent CLI has
    discoverable help, structured output, predictable errors, and a way to run without
    interactive prompts. The terminal alone doesn't provide those things.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compare">MCP can be part of the script too</h2>
  <p>
    MCP, the Model Context Protocol, standardizes how clients discover and call tools.
    It doesn't require a model to read every intermediate result or forbid a client from
    calling tools in code. A rigid menu is a limitation of that particular server and client,
    not a rule that every MCP integration must follow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Steinberger's <a href="https://github.com/openclaw/mcporter">MCPorter</a>
    makes the overlap concrete: it lets developers call MCP tools from TypeScript or package
    them as a CLI. You can keep an existing integration and change how the agent uses it.
    Anthropic's code-execution approach makes the same point from the other direction.
  </p>
  <p>
    MCP also earns its place when the client has no terminal, or when you want a shared tool
    interface across several clients. A hosted assistant with connector support shouldn't
    need a shell just to read a record. For a coding agent that already works in a terminal,
    though, a good CLI is a sensible first choice. Try the tool it can already compose with.
  </p>

  <h2 id="production">Keep the flexibility. Limit the production access.</h2>
  <p>
    There's a catch when the commands reach production. The same freedom that lets an agent
    combine three useful tools also lets it combine three dangerous ones. Giving it your
    administrator credentials because it knows how to use the CLI is still giving it your
    administrator credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let the agent write scripts and process results in its workspace. Give the remote account
    only the operations you want it to perform. An MCP tool can expose an unrestricted shell;
    a CLI can call a service that permits only specific actions. Neither name tells you how
    much authority the agent actually has. We go through that progression in <.link navigate={
      ~p"/guides/give-ai-agents-safe-production-access"
    }>how to give an AI agent safe access to production</.link>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="emisar-example">Where emisar fits</h2>
  <p>
    This is why emisar offers both interfaces. A terminal agent can use the CLI, compose
    read operations in scripts, and filter JSON results before bringing them back into the
    conversation. A client that supports MCP can call the same infrastructure actions.
    Both paths check access and apply your policy; using the CLI doesn't skip an approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    The program is called <code>emisar-mcp</code>, but it also works directly from the terminal.
    Its <.link navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference#command-line"}>CLI reference</.link>
    covers discovery, JSON output, and following an operation to its result. Give the agent
    room to write the script. Keep the rules about what it may do on your servers outside
    that script.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
